                "text": "Let me understand the rule. The verifier: objects(I, T, F, T) - univalued=True, diagonal=False, without_bg=True. Since all non-bg cells are color 5 (grey), with univalued=True and connected via dneighbors (4-connectivity), each connected component of grey cells is an object.\n\nThen objects of size 2 get colored 3 (THREE), size 3 get colored 2 (TWO), size 4 get colored 1 (ONE).\n\nWait, let's check: x1 = matcher(size, TWO=2), x2 = mfilter(objects, size==2), x7 = fill(I, THREE, x2) - so size-2 objects become color 3.\n\nx4 = size==3 objects, x8 = fill(x7, TWO, x4) - size-3 objects become color 2.\n\nx6 = size==4 objects, x9 = fill(x8, ONE, x6) - size-4 objects become color 1.\n\nSo the mapping: 2 cells -> 3 (red), 3 cells -> 2 (green)... wait the natural language says \"count the 4 grid shape and color it with blue... 2 grid with green and 3 grid with red.\" That's a bit confusing but the verifier is definitive: 2->3, 3->2, 4->1.\n\nLet me verify with example. Grey objects: \n- (1,5),(1,6),(1,7) - 3 cells horizontal \u2192 color 2. In output row 1: 2,2,2 at cols 5,6,7. \u2713\n- (3,0),(3,1),(4,0),(4,1) - 2x2 square = 4 cells \u2192 color 1. Output: rows 3,4 cols 0,1 = 1. \u2713\n- (4,6),(5,6) - 2 cells vertical \u2192 color 3. Output: (4,6)=3, (5,6)=3. \u2713\n- (7,0),(7,1),(8,0),(8,1) - 4 cells \u2192 color 1. \u2713\n\nGreat.\n\nNow I need to create 3 demonstration pairs. Input grids with grey (5) shapes, output computed by verifier.\n\nNote: objects uses univalued=True, diagonal=False, without_bg=True. bg = mostcolor(I). So the background must be the most common color. Typically 0.\n\nI need to design inputs. Each object of size 2, 3, or 4. Other sizes would... objects of other sizes just remain grey (5) in output? fill only recolors size 2,3,4 objects. So objects of size 1 or 5+ stay 5. I should probably avoid ambiguity - use only sizes 2,3,4. Actually to be faithful maybe include variety but safest to only use 2,3,4.\n\nLet me create 3 d